──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Domain-Specific Roles (no LOGIN):

  For each domain (e.g., doc_tracker, eviction_addresses, ocdc_new, oscn, etc):

    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ domain_<domain>_reader                     │  (SELECT on <domain> schema)
    └──────────────┬─────────────────────────────┘
                   │
                   ▼
    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ domain_<domain>_writer                     │  (INSERT, UPDATE on <domain> schema)
    └────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  (Each writer inherits its corresponding reader.)

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Integration Roles (LOGIN):

  For each external integration (e.g. GitHub actions, Shiny, access for other orgs, etc.):

    ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ integration_<integration_>                 │  (Inherits whatever permissions are needed for that specific purpose)
    └────────────────────────────────────────────┘

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
To create new tables / schemas / roles:

  1) Log in as all_table_owner
  2) Create new tables / schemas as needed, following general structure we already have
  3) Create new roles as needed (e.g. domain_<new domain>_reader, domain_<new domain>_writer, integration_<new thing>)
  4) Grant permissions to those roles, using existing domains as much as possible
  5) Configure default privileges for those roles via ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ...
  6) Ensure everything is documented in the script ojodb-base-config.sql and in Notion.